***artnet****
we need to redo the artnet pixel mapping workflow.
first of all it looks like casparcg has native artnet consumer.
second of all there is something baked in that can do pixel averaging (sws_scale)
third there is a improved artnet consumer, that we could build into the extended version of caspar we are using.
https://github.com/firecraftgaming/caspar-server/tree/improved-artnet

@firecraftgaming what are your thoughts on the following:

    I am basically trying to map channel output to an RGBW LED matrix. There are a number of controllers that will accept ArtNet data and map it to LED strips.

    Single universe limitation is a bummer. It only allows 128 RGBW LEDs. My idea was for it be the starting universe and allow the number of channels to extend beyond 512.

    I was thinking of making the <fixture-count> parameter accept values like 128x128 instead of specifying individual rows.

    Using <x> and <y> to specify the center of the region is a royal PITA, if you ask me. I'd like to either repurpose them to be the top-left corner (breaking change) or maybe add <top> and <left> tags for that.

    How important is the <rotation> parameter and can't that be handled by the mixer (ie, rotate the image)? The reason I am asking is that instead of doing home-grown color averaging, we can use sws_scale() function to re-scale the region of the image to the exact fixture matrix and then just grab one pixel for each fixture. The only problem is that sws_scale() doesn't do rotation.

    I'd like to be able to specify flux values for the LEDs so that RGB colors can be properly translated to the corresponding R, G, B and W values.

